WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.141
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.141 --> 00:00:06.331
Thank you so much for

00:00:06.331 --> 00:00:09.912
joining us as our first
View From The Top speaker of the year.

00:00:09.912 --> 00:00:15.286
As you can see by all this interior,
we are very excited to have you.

00:00:15.286 --> 00:00:20.697
So Dean Levin mentioned the fact
that you were born in Mexico,

00:00:20.697 --> 00:00:24.546
and you grew up as
the eldest of nine siblings

00:00:24.546 --> 00:00:29.041
in a working class family
in Southern California.

00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:30.952
How has that experience shaped you?

00:00:30.952 --> 00:00:36.881
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, well I was one
of the original Dreamers, right?

00:00:36.881 --> 00:00:38.803
Before they had cool names for it.

00:00:38.803 --> 00:00:40.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But let's be clear,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.568
they did have names for it, they weren't
very, [LAUGH] they weren't very nice.

00:00:45.568 --> 00:00:49.001
I think environment.

00:00:49.001 --> 00:00:50.403
I think how you grow up,

00:00:50.403 --> 00:00:54.482
who you grow up with has a meaningful
impact on who you are as a person.

00:00:54.482 --> 00:00:58.531
There's a lot of you in this room,
you've all had very different upbringings.

00:00:58.531 --> 00:01:02.754
And I know from my heritage,
in particular, I think the Latino

00:01:02.754 --> 00:01:07.633
population is one that's very family
oriented, humble, in general.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:11.779
And again, more importantly,
just loyal, sometimes to a fault.

00:01:11.779 --> 00:01:15.600
And that has all shaped me, and
then the hard work aspect of that.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.874
And so those are my foundations,
I didn't know to do anything else.

00:01:20.874 --> 00:01:24.119
The concept of entitlement when
you're an original Dreamer,

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:26.209
not particularly strong in your world.

00:01:26.209 --> 00:01:28.615
So it did shape me into who I am, and

00:01:28.615 --> 00:01:32.277
it does affect how I make
decisions to this very day.

00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:36.901
I think I make a lot of decisions,
and we as a corporation make a lot of

00:01:36.901 --> 00:01:41.461
decisions that are founded on some
of that upbringing that I have.

00:01:41.461 --> 00:01:44.016
&gt;&gt; And like some of the students
in the audience today,

00:01:44.016 --> 00:01:46.702
you were also the first in
your family to go to college.

00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:50.400
And you were deciding between
some great universities,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.879
the Stanford of the East,
as Dean Levin adequately put it, and USC.

00:01:54.879 --> 00:01:58.608
You chose USC,
were the Boston winters too cold?

00:01:58.608 --> 00:02:00.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.241
&gt;&gt; Well, on the West Coast,

00:02:02.241 --> 00:02:04.321
there was a better football program,

00:02:04.321 --> 00:02:06.799
at the time,
[LAUGH] clearly not the case now.

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:09.389
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, you know what,

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:14.180
the story behind that is, again, being
in a first-generation, which I hear some

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.370
of you in the room are first-generation
undergrads, raise your hand.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:22.954
Huge respect, guys, it's a big
responsibility for you and your families.

00:02:22.954 --> 00:02:27.266
But not knowing, I mean, literally, the
way I decided that I had to go to college,

00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:30.839
I was walking down the hallway late
to a class, and Mrs. Duckworth,

00:02:30.839 --> 00:02:34.562
one of my high school counselors,
stopped me and said, are you Oscar?

00:02:34.562 --> 00:02:36.227
And it's like, crap, I'm in trouble.

00:02:36.227 --> 00:02:37.339
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:37.339 --> 00:02:38.220
&gt;&gt; because why else do high school

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.271
counselors stop you, right?

00:02:39.271 --> 00:02:41.316
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We had just taken those PSATs, or

00:02:41.316 --> 00:02:44.255
whatever, and she goes, where are you
thinking of going to college?

00:02:44.255 --> 00:02:49.210
And my answer was,
honest to goodness, what's a college?

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:53.445
My upbringing, my neighborhood,
it wasn't just the Latino thing,

00:02:53.445 --> 00:02:55.112
it was just where we lived.

00:02:55.112 --> 00:02:56.346
It just wasn't something you did.

00:02:56.346 --> 00:03:00.240
You went high school, you got married,
you had kids, and you lived happily ever

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.522
after, and that was great, but
this offered me insight and a view.

00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:10.440
And to this day, I hold her in high degree
of respect for taking me down that path.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.627
She helped me with applications and
all sorts of things to get there, and

00:03:13.627 --> 00:03:15.365
that's how I decided to go to college.

00:03:15.365 --> 00:03:17.404
Now the choice of where and where,

00:03:17.404 --> 00:03:22.269
that was just my story of when I went to
the other school, was just hilarious joke.

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:24.417
I mean, talk about two country rubes.

00:03:24.417 --> 00:03:26.759
I grew up in Huntington Beach,
I was a surfer kid, right?

00:03:26.759 --> 00:03:29.979
I mean, imagine Harvard Yard and
somebody in shorts,

00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:34.611
flip-flops, a T-shirt, and hair down
to here [LAUGH] with a blue collar dad.

00:03:34.611 --> 00:03:37.956
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Even then, at a young age,

00:03:37.956 --> 00:03:41.076
I knew that you have to find your way,
find your spot, and go there, and

00:03:41.076 --> 00:03:42.711
that just didn't feel like the way.

00:03:42.711 --> 00:03:45.692
And no regrets, that was it, SC was great.

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:50.276
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
you had a heart attack 38 days

00:03:50.276 --> 00:03:54.608
after taking over as the CEO of United.

00:03:54.608 --> 00:03:55.396
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:55.396 --> 00:03:56.727
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I love this.

00:03:56.727 --> 00:03:57.815
&gt;&gt; Gotta cover the personal.

00:03:57.815 --> 00:03:58.598
Gotta cover the personal.

00:03:58.598 --> 00:04:00.832
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,
that was interesting.

00:04:00.832 --> 00:04:01.553
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And you then had a heart

00:04:01.553 --> 00:04:02.326
[INAUDIBLE] right?

00:04:02.326 --> 00:04:04.230
&gt;&gt; As a career thing,
I don't recommend that.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.731
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:07.731 --> 00:04:08.999
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and then you would have

00:04:08.999 --> 00:04:11.396
a heart transplant on your
birthday in early 2016.

00:04:11.396 --> 00:04:16.784
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so this Saturday night, a couple
of Mondays ago, the 15th of October,

00:04:16.784 --> 00:04:21.239
marked my third anniversary with
having the actual heart attack.

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:25.532
We don't celebrate it as much,
but it gets raised.

00:04:25.532 --> 00:04:30.273
It's an emotional day
because you realize what you

00:04:30.273 --> 00:04:35.131
have versus what could've been,
like not be here.

00:04:35.131 --> 00:04:38.650
This coming Saturday night,
my transplant doctor, my cardiologist, and

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.284
some of my very close friends, and
of course, my family, were celebrating.

00:04:42.284 --> 00:04:44.197
Elton John is going on his last tour,

00:04:44.197 --> 00:04:46.985
and so we're celebrating
something on that regard.

00:04:46.985 --> 00:04:51.107
But yeah, that was crazy.

00:04:51.107 --> 00:04:56.714
Life is, and all of our plans, and all the
things that you're doing are wonderful.

00:04:56.714 --> 00:05:02.122
I'm telling you, I know you hear all these
axioms, and all these superlatives and

00:05:02.122 --> 00:05:07.233
broad about live life to its fullest,
and BOLO, YOLO, and all that.

00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:08.629
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:09.765
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You know what,

00:05:09.765 --> 00:05:12.107
there's a degree of truth to those things.

00:05:12.107 --> 00:05:15.135
You never going to know what's going to
happen, and that was my situation.

00:05:15.135 --> 00:05:20.735
But yeah, 37 days, and
then the transplant on my birthday.

00:05:20.735 --> 00:05:23.378
There's a degree of fortunate,
of blessings,

00:05:23.378 --> 00:05:26.920
of being lucky that are important in life,
and I am all of those and

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.931
damned glad to be here, as they say.

00:05:28.931 --> 00:05:34.497
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so how have your perspectives and
behaviors changed day-to-day?

00:05:34.497 --> 00:05:36.441
&gt;&gt; They really haven't.

00:05:36.441 --> 00:05:38.975
I think I do appreciate life,
in general, more.

00:05:38.975 --> 00:05:42.133
And there's moments of sort
of personal emotion and

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:46.939
drama when I celebrate anniversaries like
that, or when I have to tell a story or

00:05:46.939 --> 00:05:51.990
I have to visit someone in the hospital
that's going through something like that.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:56.347
It's not only a physical issue with you
personally, it also is emotional, and

00:05:56.347 --> 00:05:58.379
then it impacts all your loved ones.

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:02.264
And it's important, I think,
to keep a very positive attitude.

00:06:02.264 --> 00:06:04.351
So all of that, I do a lot more.

00:06:04.351 --> 00:06:07.316
I go to hospitals, literally,
all over the world, and I'll see folks and

00:06:07.316 --> 00:06:07.892
talk to them.

00:06:07.892 --> 00:06:11.782
Just by the nature of what I do in
my usual energetic rambling self,

00:06:11.782 --> 00:06:14.635
people look, it's like hey,
he looks normal.

00:06:14.635 --> 00:06:19.395
And when they know what I've been through,
it's a bit of a motivator for people to

00:06:19.395 --> 00:06:24.227
get out of their funk because, I'm telling
you, doctors are very conservative,

00:06:24.227 --> 00:06:27.747
and they'll tell you all of
these things you shouldn't do,

00:06:27.747 --> 00:06:32.257
like I shouldn't be in any enclosed
spaces with a lot of people, airplanes.

00:06:32.257 --> 00:06:35.369
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I shouldn't be hanging around with

00:06:35.369 --> 00:06:39.135
a bunch of [INAUDIBLE] It's like
everything I'm not supposed to do,

00:06:39.135 --> 00:06:41.529
I couldn't possibly live without doing.

00:06:41.529 --> 00:06:44.643
And that's my choice, and
my doctors generally keep up with me.

00:06:44.643 --> 00:06:47.646
But it is an important part of, again,

00:06:47.646 --> 00:06:53.662
back to the question you raised earlier
with regards to who you are as a person.

00:06:53.662 --> 00:06:56.668
Things like that should shape you and
change you, but who I am,

00:06:56.668 --> 00:06:59.407
how I approach things,
that had nothing to do with that.

00:06:59.407 --> 00:07:02.083
And they asked, all my loved ones,
have I changed?

00:07:02.083 --> 00:07:06.514
And they're like no, you're still the same
nut crazy person that I've always been.

00:07:06.514 --> 00:07:11.386
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I want to talk about
sort of how your personality, and

00:07:11.386 --> 00:07:16.741
that thinking, and that process led
you to take the role at United.

00:07:16.741 --> 00:07:18.461
So when you took over as CEO,

00:07:18.461 --> 00:07:22.624
you inherited a company that was
still struggling to overcome some of

00:07:22.624 --> 00:07:25.736
the challenges from
the merger with Continental.

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:31.295
And it's not common for airline CEOs
to come from outside of the industry.

00:07:31.295 --> 00:07:34.457
Why did you believe it was
the right job for you?

00:07:34.457 --> 00:07:37.997
&gt;&gt; I think it's back to my fundamental
foundation of who I am and

00:07:37.997 --> 00:07:41.063
what I think I can do, and
advice I give to all of you.

00:07:41.063 --> 00:07:46.315
You really have to know who you are,
it's the old know thyself thing.

00:07:46.315 --> 00:07:48.784
And I always did, know thyself, comma, and

00:07:48.784 --> 00:07:52.364
then I would write a really big
really with an exclamation point.

00:07:52.364 --> 00:07:56.907
Not the self that you think and you
want to project to others that you are,

00:07:56.907 --> 00:07:57.901
the real self.

00:07:57.901 --> 00:08:01.378
The real self in the middle of
the night and your mind's going,

00:08:01.378 --> 00:08:06.500
we are all Petty and jealous and
greedy and angry, all of those things.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.110
Those are pure, wonderful human qualities.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:13.310
The more you understand yourself, the more
you know your predilection in those areas,

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:16.190
I'm telling you, it's the best of
yourselves when you know that.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:19.190
I think part of maturity,
I think part of growing up,

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:22.740
I think part of having success, you're
able to have these conversations and

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.720
I'm telling you, know yourself.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:25.460
Really!

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.860
And when you do, and so
back to the application of me.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.560
I know what I love, I know what I like.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.680
I love the work that we're
doing with the railroad.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:33.890
But it was a railroad.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.530
It was a B2B, it was freight.

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.110
It was very industrial,
very labor oriented.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:40.500
We'd take it, we had great runs

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.710
as the dean mentioned with regards
to what we had done financially.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:45.440
But there was always something missing for
me.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.930
And I miss the things from my Coca-Cola
and Pepsi cola days, branding mechanism,

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:52.060
marketing, connecting with other humans.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.570
I thought that was really important.

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:54.760
And, more importantly,

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.970
being on the board, I saw some of
the frustration in our employee base.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:01.640
I saw some of the frustration
with our customers.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.860
And I certainly saw where,
again, back to knowing myself.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.030
What do I get the most energy from?

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.210
Well, it is capturing the hearts and
minds of many,

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.940
many folks in a large organization,
and I'm thinking, I can do that.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:16.860
And then I had a couple board members
who were really instrumental in

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.120
another sort of initiative,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:22.470
objective of mine in my life, is how
do you make a difference in this world?

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:26.900
And from what platform can you make it,
and we all have different platforms, and

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.020
we can all do different
goods in different areas.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:33.166
But taking a big brand like United,
driving the turn around,

00:09:33.166 --> 00:09:37.165
having success,
gives you a platform to really advocate.

00:09:37.165 --> 00:09:40.414
To really do good things for
the community, do good things for

00:09:40.414 --> 00:09:42.300
the environment, for you name it.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:45.520
I now have a voice on a platform
that I wouldn't have without that.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.195
And one of our board members,
a guy named Walter Isaacson,

00:09:48.195 --> 00:09:52.370
you guys may have heard of him, arguably
the best living American writer today.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.790
He called me and for two hours, he just
berated about taking this role because

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.790
I was literally going to be the CEO
of this next company, like in days.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:02.450
And he's like,
you have the opportunity, Oscar,

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:05.850
to change the face of American capitalism.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:11.058
And that's only something like
an author of his renown would say,

00:10:11.058 --> 00:10:14.409
and I'm like, well dang, that's okay.

00:10:14.409 --> 00:10:16.455
I was just hoping to get free flights or
something.

00:10:16.455 --> 00:10:20.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But when he said that,

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.830
and it's actually sparked a lot
of conversations that I've had,

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.620
not only with my employees,
but in general.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.660
I like to say things like,

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:31.130
we're building a company that's not
only profitable, but principled.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:32.360
And that you can be both.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:33.360
And more importantly,

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.200
that by being principled,
you can actually be even more profitable.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.330
And as you guys get into your career,

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:44.635
as you're starting mid or beginning to
reemerge from school back into the work.

00:10:44.635 --> 00:10:47.945
It is becoming an ever-increasing
level of attention from

00:10:47.945 --> 00:10:50.095
the new generation that you bring, right?

00:10:50.095 --> 00:10:52.265
It's not just the product,
it's not the what you do,

00:10:52.265 --> 00:10:55.200
like we do in but
it's also the how you do it.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.540
And I think I can do both.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:00.360
And so that was an opportunity that
I think I had at United that I may or

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.030
may not have had, really, at CSX.

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:06.388
because you probably wouldn't have me here
if I was at CSX, just saying, I think.

00:11:06.388 --> 00:11:08.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, Dr.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:09.658
Parker knows me, so maybe I would be.

00:11:09.658 --> 00:11:10.860
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I want to

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.190
come back to the platform
discussion in a little bit,

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:18.190
but the phrase that you used, the road to
changing the face of American capitalism.

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:22.387
That road hasn't been the smoothest road,
aside from the personal challenges.

00:11:22.387 --> 00:11:23.141
&gt;&gt; Turbulence, we call it.

00:11:23.141 --> 00:11:27.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Turbulence, yes, exactly.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:30.900
There were a series of
incidents last year,

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.630
including the forced removal of
a passenger on a United flight, and

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:39.220
separately, the death of
animals on United flights.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:43.960
And I guess what I'm curious about is,

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:49.600
what lessons, you've publicly acknowledged
and apologized for this on many occasion.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.780
But for the students, what lessons have
you learned through that experience?

00:11:55.790 --> 00:12:00.530
&gt;&gt; First and foremost, and when we talked
before hand with Liz and Patrick, I said,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.600
there's nothing off the table.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.380
Ask me whatever the heck you want, period.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.595
That's like, there is nothing that I'm,
anything of value,

00:12:08.595 --> 00:12:10.566
because everything we do is learning.

00:12:10.566 --> 00:12:12.520
There is turbulence in
everything that we do.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:17.587
That situation has sparked what I believe
will be the major cog in United's

00:12:17.587 --> 00:12:22.186
turn around over time is that it
got our collective stuff together.

00:12:22.186 --> 00:12:26.204
We are such a big company with
big operational requirements,

00:12:26.204 --> 00:12:30.770
where safety and security have to
be our topmost objectives, right?

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:32.510
Has to be.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:36.430
Policies and procedures are rigorous,
they're demanding, and more importantly,

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:39.340
they're constrictive and
they don't give you a lot of room for

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:42.630
what we're trying to build now,
which is a caring culture.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.210
And so in that particular instance,
the learning was, we let our policies and

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.920
procedures get in the way of doing
the damn right thing, right?

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.600
Who in their right mind would bring the
police, and we all saw the video, right?

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:59.028
I mean, 500 million views in China,
I was out visiting and

00:12:59.028 --> 00:13:03.216
I'm like,
that doesn't teach you something, it does.

00:13:06.025 --> 00:13:09.960
But it was a catalyst to what we've
established from that point forward.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.450
We've established so
many more customer oriented.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:16.460
Our philosophy and our next journey after
we've done what we've done so far is,

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:21.680
I want to make you, we want to make
you feel better about flying us.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:22.910
What does that mean?

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.550
Put that in your spreadsheet.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:29.900
You can't, but you can because if you
think how we built this company to today

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:33.430
the first thing I did is I regained
the trust of my employees.

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:36.837
That was the first big
saying to Wall Street.

00:13:36.837 --> 00:13:40.373
And Wall Street went just apoplectic,
like, what the hell was that?

00:13:40.373 --> 00:13:43.237
He was from outside industry,
doesn't know what he is doing and

00:13:43.237 --> 00:13:45.740
the first thing he says,
he's going to be nice to people?

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:49.630
And so, what's the return on that?

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:51.100
Well, you fast forward a year later,

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.280
our operations are,
we settled our union contracts.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:54.920
Our operations are doing best.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:59.290
We've led the last two years
in an on time originations.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:01.240
We're getting our planes out on time.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.620
And we haven't invested
a huge amount of money.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:05.800
We've invested in human capital, right?

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:06.590
And getting those trust.

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:10.180
So build the trust of employees,
get that right, build your operations.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.050
Then build a strategy,
a business strategy.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:13.490
Which all of you love, right?

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:15.160
That's the sexy part.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:19.266
That sexy part, it don't work without
the human capital underneath it.

00:14:19.266 --> 00:14:22.479
And that's another thing that we
always forget, is that, trust me,

00:14:22.479 --> 00:14:24.440
in that large of an organization that's so

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:28.410
spread all over God's green earth,
literally, because we're a global company.

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.450
You have to get that right and
then build from that.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.160
That's the foundation for
the things that we've been doing and

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.370
this next journey is about the customer
and making you feel better.

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:40.650
That will probably be the most arduous and
difficult thing,

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:42.380
because it's hard to measure.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.130
You tell me everyday.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:49.460
And you can tell me, I mean MPS is such
a hard thing because if your coffee wasn't

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:55.130
the right temperature, if somebody sitting
next to you had a dog that was the size of

00:14:55.130 --> 00:15:00.075
Bosnia, I'm in again trouble for
saying, nothing against Bosnia.

00:15:00.075 --> 00:15:02.560
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But there's saving,

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:06.070
all of you are frequent fliers, you all
have your views about what's right,

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:09.850
we're chatting in the back about, if I
ever write a book, I'm going to title it,

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:12.920
there is no good deed that goes
unpunished in this business.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:15.130
because if I do something for
customers, Wall Street gets upset.

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:18.630
If I do something for Wall Street,
employees and customers get upset.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:23.070
So it's always this constant thing and
you're a demanding, wonderful crowd.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.070
And so my thing I would tell you is that

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.000
people like to catch you in these kind of
conversations, especially on national TV.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.110
They will ask you don't you think
the customer is always right?

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:35.370
And I said, you know what, unfortunately
in this day and age, safety and

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:36.930
security are paramount.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:38.690
And to the extent that we can,

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.990
after taking care of those two, we want to
put you at the center of everything we do.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:44.810
And that's a difficult task, but
you have to understand, safety and

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:45.710
security are key.

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:48.120
I'd love to have you just
show up at the airport and

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:49.180
get on any damn plane you want.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:51.220
Yeah, come on in, get on.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:52.558
Southwest does that, I think but.

00:15:52.558 --> 00:15:55.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:00.200
He's going to come after me.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:06.020
[LAUGH] So, gosh, we've got so
much to do still.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:10.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and how do you balance
That what you just discussed.

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:14.710
So investing in a better customer
experience while maintaining the cost

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:18.390
discipline required for
profitability in the short term.

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:21.280
Expectations from Wall Street in
a highly competitive industry,

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:22.230
how do you balance that?

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:26.160
&gt;&gt; See, this is where these
are the things that we need

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.510
to learn more of even at
this relatively young age.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:34.690
So I call that debate NPS
net promoter versus EPS,

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:37.670
because in our world that's the tradeoff.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:40.110
I mean sometimes you just
have this decision where

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:41.680
do I put more seats in the airplane?

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:45.895
Well you don't like that, but
density, and overhead absorption, and

00:16:45.895 --> 00:16:48.885
all the things that we
know makes sense for us.

00:16:48.885 --> 00:16:52.495
So how do we balance those two things,
well they're trade offs.

00:16:52.495 --> 00:16:56.425
And more importantly for us at United,
what we're doing is we're listening to you

00:16:56.425 --> 00:16:58.625
our customers,
like what's important to you?

00:16:58.625 --> 00:17:01.025
Does this matter to you,
does it really, how do we do this?

00:17:01.025 --> 00:17:04.085
And then when we roll things out,
we test it with a lot of different folks.

00:17:04.085 --> 00:17:06.300
We just introduced something
called better boarding.

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.120
And you're like,
how boring is that, right?

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:10.190
Boarding, really?

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:11.570
Well it's one of
the highest stress points.

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:12.700
How do I know that?

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:13.230
I asked you.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:17.390
And it's going like gangbusters.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:18.730
Well you went from five to two and

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.710
everybody's gone from two to three and
a half.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:22.210
Everybody's done this all the time.

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:23.830
But they haven't done the secret sauce.

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:27.290
And why ours is working is I spent,
my team spent eight months

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:30.590
working with customers and
with gate agents who do what?

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.620
They do this for
a living every single day.

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:36.250
They know how things board and
what works and what doesn't so

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:38.450
our really smart people with the flows and

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:41.405
the regressions and the statistical
analysis that says this or that.

00:17:42.905 --> 00:17:47.635
The first sign of a plan first falls
apart at the sign of the enemy and

00:17:47.635 --> 00:17:50.095
the enemy is our own people,
it's like this is stupid.

00:17:50.095 --> 00:17:52.566
This doesn't work,
why don't we try this one or we try that.

00:17:52.566 --> 00:17:57.120
And so the concept of involving
folks is it requires patience.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:00.575
It requires you putting your ego and
your intelligence off to the side.

00:18:00.575 --> 00:18:03.615
You're all going to come out of
here with a Stanford degree got,

00:18:03.615 --> 00:18:05.650
you guys have had a massive leg up.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:10.610
But if you go back into your workplaces,
what I call, all asses and elbows, right?

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.440
Elbowing people out of the way
because you're so damn smart.

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:19.610
I'm telling you, one of the things that
we learn as we gain more experience.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:24.230
One of the saddest things that you have
is middle to senior middle managers who

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:29.820
lament the fact that their careers
have not gone like they planned.

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.960
And they look around at everybody else.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:34.960
I'm so smart, I'm so capable.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.984
No one recognizes that.

00:18:36.984 --> 00:18:40.320
Ladies and gentlemen, I'm telling you
it is a meritocratic world out there.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:41.710
People notice quality.

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.060
People notice leadership.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:44.830
People reward it to this very day.

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:47.950
There's pockets with bias,
and racial things, and

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:50.170
then sex, all of that stuff, no question.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:51.020
But in general,

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:55.960
as a whole, people can get ahead because
you made an effort to get ahead.

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:59.700
All of those axioms about treating others,
and not having an agenda and working,

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:00.930
all that sort of thing.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:03.830
But it's the saddest thing when
people are at that age and

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:07.350
then they just haven't figured
out the EQ side of the thing.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:08.950
You have to make yourself
the kind of person

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:13.420
that people are willing to come up to and
provide advice.

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:19.070
I run a really big company, and you'd be
appalled at some of the conversations

00:19:19.070 --> 00:19:21.690
that people have with me
when someone's upset.

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:23.740
They will yell at me,

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:28.250
they'll stand there, and they'll do this,
and I am loving every minute of it.

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:32.375
Because at the end of the day,
sharing is caring, right?

00:19:32.375 --> 00:19:34.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And if you're yelling at me I still

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:39.090
have a shot at you, it's like, and that
has made the biggest difference in our

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.080
company because they know
they can have that output.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:45.170
And then once you do it you begin
to capture hearts and minds.

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.660
And then when they ask them to do it,

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:51.560
we ask ourselves to do things it's
a lot easier way of doing it so

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:56.486
it's a long question to one I don't
even remember what you asked.

00:19:56.486 --> 00:19:59.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's okay, so

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:05.495
I do have to admit to the audience that I
am a United loyalist and status holder.

00:20:05.495 --> 00:20:09.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I know, I know.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.500
I know, firsthand, how a motive-
&gt;&gt; He's from Boston.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:15.408
The first thing why do you
have a hyphenated last name.

00:20:15.408 --> 00:20:18.538
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That was actually his first question

00:20:18.538 --> 00:20:19.298
to me.

00:20:19.298 --> 00:20:22.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Another trick to interviews,

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:23.286
always turn the tables.

00:20:23.286 --> 00:20:23.960
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:28.110
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I do know, firsthand, how a motive and

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:33.210
even tribal people can be about
their airlines However, some

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:38.530
have had as we've discussed, some very
negative experiences with United and some

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:42.650
have even stated that they would quote,
unquote, never fly the airline again.

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:43.670
Why should they reconsider?

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:49.490
&gt;&gt; A funny thing, so over time you get
to know people everywhere and some of

00:20:49.490 --> 00:20:54.180
the SNL cast I become friendly with and
everybody knows Weekend Update, right?

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:59.600
If you go back to the time when
they reported on the DOW incident.

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:04.592
That's the flight where we had to,
a bad flight.

00:21:04.592 --> 00:21:07.805
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Earlier in the week,

00:21:07.805 --> 00:21:10.840
somebody had found a scorpion
in one of our overhead bins.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:14.500
And so, he leads with this.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:19.290
And tonight on the news, United Airlines
found a scorpion on the thing.

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:22.512
And then he kind of smiles and
he's thinking, honestly,

00:21:22.512 --> 00:21:25.356
that wasn't the worst news of the week for
United.

00:21:25.356 --> 00:21:28.339
And they were brief and just mentioned
that, they were very nice to us,

00:21:28.339 --> 00:21:31.410
they just mentioned it briefly
as they do for everyone else.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.190
And then he stares into the camera,
and he goes I will never fly, and

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:36.070
it's with a serious face, pause, pause,

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:40.655
until they have a good price to wherever I
want to go, then of course I'll fly there.

00:21:40.655 --> 00:21:45.604
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There's a guy that use to

00:21:45.604 --> 00:21:50.828
run Spirit Airlines which he-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:50.828 --> 00:21:54.240
&gt;&gt; He would carry around these 12 letters

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:55.790
and he would open one of the letter and

00:21:55.790 --> 00:21:57.980
it would be somebody saying
I'll never fly this airline.

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:01.540
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; None of this and the remaining 11

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:05.920
letters were from the same customer
over the course of two or three years.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:08.050
The economic impact,

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:12.070
and the price of things makes a difference
to all of us, there's no question.

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:13.910
You may not fly someone again or whatever.

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:17.650
People are very emotional about this
business, it's very personal to them,

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:20.950
which is why our purpose and
mission is about connecting people.

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:25.060
Not the physical connection from point
A to point B, but connecting you,

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:28.750
as a human being, to something that's
really important to you somewhere else,

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:30.810
and that's the foundation
of our shared purpose.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:35.190
Then again, back to my previous comment,
involve 90,000 people, telling me,

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:37.040
telling us, what was important to them.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:43.730
So connecting people in that more
personal way, and then uniting the world.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:47.990
Which is an interesting thing that came
out because it's really led me and

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.880
us to be able to do a lot of.

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:55.790
Take stands in DACA, Dreamers,
the separation of children.

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:59.300
Gun control and some of the issues that
surround that, because when you have

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:02.460
a shared purpose that's uniting the world
that your 90,000 employees had told you,

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:04.900
it gives you a responsibility
to take action.

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:07.040
And take a position on
some of those things.

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:12.190
&gt;&gt; So I want to transition into speaking
about some of the management and

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:15.640
leadership challenges, and some of
the things that you've done recently.

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:19.760
Many do not realize that
the flight attending crews from

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:24.980
the United Continental merger continued
to work separately for over eight years.

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:28.270
That is, until earlier this month
when you successfully finalized

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:30.170
the deal with the unions.

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:32.700
How did you succeed in
reaching a deal after so

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.840
long, and can you share some
negotiating tips with us?

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:42.230
&gt;&gt; So the actual contractual deal
was signed a couple years ago when

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:43.610
we agreed on the contract.

00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:46.166
Implementing the contract
was what just transpired.

00:23:46.166 --> 00:23:50.790
&gt;&gt; You got 26,000 people,
flight attendants who are the front,

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:53.760
the face, the brand to some degree.

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:55.560
Coming from two different cultures.

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:58.759
All of you will study mergers and
acquisitions, we'll do all the math,

00:23:58.759 --> 00:24:00.606
the synergies, how cost can be.

00:24:00.606 --> 00:24:02.921
All the things that we like to do.

00:24:02.921 --> 00:24:06.877
The human integration of different
cultures Along with the systems that

00:24:06.877 --> 00:24:10.430
support them are very critical in how you
handle those and how you manage them.

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:13.870
Because there's so much pride and
so much more history and so

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:18.128
much more resistance to change in those
kind of mergers and integrations.

00:24:18.128 --> 00:24:19.185
So it took us a while.

00:24:19.185 --> 00:24:21.720
The contract had been going on for
five years.

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:28.860
And when I came, it's just my style, this
is where history will always follow you.

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:31.840
So I had spent ten plus
years at a railroad

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.030
where we were largely union oriented.

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:38.210
And unions tend be headed by the same
people across on a national level.

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:41.598
When a union from one company,
when you switch to another company,

00:24:41.598 --> 00:24:45.282
calls the union people at that other
company and say, that's a good guy,

00:24:45.282 --> 00:24:46.669
he's going to work with you.

00:24:46.669 --> 00:24:51.030
Huge, huge benefit to
starting a calculation.

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:52.246
So I don't make that up.

00:24:52.246 --> 00:24:52.840
I don't do that.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:54.340
It's just the way I work.

00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:58.076
And the way you work with people,
now, my dad was a union meat cutter.

00:24:58.076 --> 00:25:00.870
My uncle was head of
the IBEW in California.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:03.780
Started unions in Los Angeles,

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:07.170
literally when people were getting
killed for organizing things.

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:08.681
So I grew up in that environment.

00:25:08.681 --> 00:25:10.510
I've seen the value of it to some degree,

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:12.929
I also see some of the deficiencies
as we move forward.

00:25:12.929 --> 00:25:16.086
But nevertheless, it's the world
they've chosen and you have to admire,

00:25:16.086 --> 00:25:19.530
you have to respect it, more importantly
you have to acknowledge it, right?

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:23.250
Seek first to understand,
then to be understood.

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:24.712
And we don't do that, you're a union guy,

00:25:24.712 --> 00:25:26.780
you clearly you don't have
my best interest in mind.

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:28.507
You know what?
Try it.

00:25:28.507 --> 00:25:30.823
And I think once you've built
that little bit of that history,

00:25:30.823 --> 00:25:32.451
it made a difference
when we came to United.

00:25:32.451 --> 00:25:36.200
And we settled that five year contract
in one year and it wasn't usurious.

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:38.608
It wasn't giving everything away.

00:25:38.608 --> 00:25:42.091
It was very fair contract for both,
which is the way it should be, right?

00:25:42.091 --> 00:25:45.270
A fair day's pay for a fair day's work.

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:48.315
And the only thing that needs to be
negotiated is what's a fair day and

00:25:48.315 --> 00:25:49.164
what's fair pay.

00:25:49.164 --> 00:25:53.265
[LAUGH] That, ladies and gentlemen,
many books written on that.

00:25:53.265 --> 00:25:58.235
But having a relationship to start
the conversation is much easier than not,

00:25:58.235 --> 00:25:59.003
in my mind.

00:25:59.003 --> 00:26:03.342
&gt;&gt; So being located in Silicon Valley,
Stanford students are often drawn into

00:26:03.342 --> 00:26:08.630
the start-up and the technology
companies in this area after graduation.

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:11.450
But why should we consider working for
a more established company,

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:12.640
such as United, instead?

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.760
&gt;&gt; Well again,
back to the concept of know thyself.

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:19.690
What makes you tick?

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:22.021
If you're just following, [COUGH] again,

00:26:22.021 --> 00:26:26.243
I have to be careful because my children
have all become bankers and consultants.

00:26:26.243 --> 00:26:29.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Not that anybody wants to do that here.

00:26:29.470 --> 00:26:34.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And tech is a great space.

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:35.885
And if you're good at it,
you really love it, and

00:26:35.885 --> 00:26:37.002
it's something you really want to do?

00:26:37.002 --> 00:26:42.400
And it really motivates you, and
gets you going, go do that thing.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:45.935
The sooner you learn that what you love,
and what you want to do, and

00:26:45.935 --> 00:26:48.472
what makes you passionate,
and you go do that?

00:26:48.472 --> 00:26:51.840
I'm telling you, you're young,
you have lots of life left.

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:55.730
You go do something just for the money,
just because what's your friend,

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:57.260
that's just currently what's hot?

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:59.520
You'll wake up some time in that career,
and

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:03.160
say, dang it,
I wish I had done something else.

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:06.810
So again, back to,
nobody knows yourself better than you.

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:10.210
So think hard about those things and
if that's what you want to do, wonderful,

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:10.970
go do it.

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:12.330
It's all great training ground.

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:13.640
But sometime between then,

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:16.410
it's kind of like choosing
a major in undergrad, all right?

00:27:16.410 --> 00:27:17.970
Go try all the different things.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:21.903
But at some point in time the school calls
you, the dean calls you and says, dude,

00:27:21.903 --> 00:27:22.712
you gotta pick.

00:27:22.712 --> 00:27:27.137
Life won't tell you that, life will
let you go on, but at some point in

00:27:27.137 --> 00:27:31.937
time your personal well being, how you
reflect with the people around you,

00:27:31.937 --> 00:27:34.723
how you're production to your community.

00:27:34.723 --> 00:27:35.724
And more importantly,

00:27:35.724 --> 00:27:38.725
how you interact with your loved ones,
and you all all have loved ones,

00:27:38.725 --> 00:27:42.049
it makes a meaningful difference if
you're doing something you really like.

00:27:42.049 --> 00:27:44.733
I love what I do.

00:27:44.733 --> 00:27:47.098
I won't say, it's a very difficult job,

00:27:47.098 --> 00:27:50.792
[LAUGH] because of all the factors
that we face, all over the world.

00:27:50.792 --> 00:27:53.900
There's not a single exogenous factor
that doesn't affect an industry like us.

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:55.270
We're always working with that.

00:27:55.270 --> 00:27:58.835
But you're right, my foundation is the
energy that I derive from my people and

00:27:58.835 --> 00:28:00.033
that's what I love to do.

00:28:00.033 --> 00:28:02.187
That's why I chose to come here from CSX.

00:28:02.187 --> 00:28:03.333
That's why I choose to continue.

00:28:03.333 --> 00:28:06.555
That's why I chose to come back
from dang I almost died, right?

00:28:06.555 --> 00:28:09.568
I mean, who comes back from a heart
transplant to do this stuff?

00:28:09.568 --> 00:28:11.904
Well again, it's just who I am.

00:28:11.904 --> 00:28:15.659
And it's good to know that because you'll
have decisions in your life that will

00:28:15.659 --> 00:28:18.070
require you to make some tough decisions.

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:21.940
You can go read all the books and
talk to all your wonderful professors.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:25.783
All of the advisors, all the counseling
you could possibly get, but man,

00:28:25.783 --> 00:28:27.253
it always comes back to you.

00:28:27.253 --> 00:28:29.421
[LAUGH] And so and it's great,

00:28:29.421 --> 00:28:33.850
you're the best at it about
making decisions for yourself.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:37.831
&gt;&gt; But this is still Silicon Valley, so

00:28:37.831 --> 00:28:40.561
I do have to ask a question
about the future.

00:28:40.561 --> 00:28:44.389
You have worked in travel and
transportation for many years now, and

00:28:44.389 --> 00:28:48.679
my question is, what do you think will
be different about the transportation

00:28:48.679 --> 00:28:50.969
industry, say 20 years from now?

00:28:50.969 --> 00:28:54.320
&gt;&gt; Gosh, it'll be completely
radically changed, right?

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:57.820
I mean, from, I hosted Dara from Uber and

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:01.829
a guy named Dennis Muilenburg
who runs Boeing.

00:29:01.829 --> 00:29:04.733
You guys know Dara and Oscar?

00:29:04.733 --> 00:29:06.630
There's some first name
people out there and so.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:09.439
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Mostly because Dara's last name is

00:29:09.439 --> 00:29:10.935
literally unpronouncable.

00:29:10.935 --> 00:29:15.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I had them both at an event

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:19.100
down in San Diego, we talked about
the future of transportation.

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:22.252
And when you hear them talk
about airplanes that fly

00:29:22.252 --> 00:29:26.430
in space to literally flying cars,
one's building the software,

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:29.890
one's building the actual vehicle,
they can do that.

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:34.044
So the Jetson's, somebody remember
those cartoons a long time ago.

00:29:34.044 --> 00:29:35.742
I've read about them, I just, somebody.

00:29:35.742 --> 00:29:39.215
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so there's just so

00:29:39.215 --> 00:29:42.379
much technology that's
going to be in our future.

00:29:42.379 --> 00:29:45.850
I think the way I approach
technology is two-fold.

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:50.598
One, whatever's new, whatever's hot,
whatever's applicable to us, let's roll.

00:29:50.598 --> 00:29:52.710
I mean we've got Palantir
here in the valley.

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:54.820
We came out and
visited about a year and a half ago and

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:58.480
interviewed all sorts of different
folks with all this great technology.

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:02.470
Big data, artificial intel, all those
wonderful buzzwords, they can do that.

00:30:02.470 --> 00:30:04.549
I'm looking for things that are relevant.

00:30:04.549 --> 00:30:07.997
It's what I call it relevant innovation or
relevant technology,

00:30:07.997 --> 00:30:10.527
that's relevant to you,
my customer, today.

00:30:10.527 --> 00:30:14.029
50 years from now, 10 years from now,
there'll be something more.

00:30:14.029 --> 00:30:17.161
I don't want to wait for you for 5,
or 10, or 50 years to be my customer.

00:30:17.161 --> 00:30:18.730
I want to you to be my customer now.

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:22.833
So what is it that I can do that's
really healthy and worthwhile for you?

00:30:22.833 --> 00:30:28.056
So I have things, we know so
much about you, in a non-creepy way.

00:30:28.056 --> 00:30:31.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I know all your flights.

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:34.690
I know whether you've been upgraded,
I know how your flight went.

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:38.601
And so I have an app and
when I sit next to you, I know all that.

00:30:38.601 --> 00:30:40.310
I know your last five flights.

00:30:40.310 --> 00:30:42.791
And I know everybody is sitting around and
before I get on an aircraft.

00:30:42.791 --> 00:30:45.398
I check everyone and
what I see is red, yellow, green.

00:30:45.398 --> 00:30:46.695
It's as simple as that.

00:30:46.695 --> 00:30:48.930
And it says, here's a red.

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:54.064
Hey, you flew from San Francisco back
to Chicago a month and a half ago, sir.

00:30:54.064 --> 00:30:59.251
Sorry about that, do you want to tell me,
and so I bring it up.

00:30:59.251 --> 00:31:03.187
And they're like, whoa,
I don't even remember the flight, but

00:31:03.187 --> 00:31:05.020
you start a conversation.

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:08.472
But the fact, it's that
personalization that's important.

00:31:08.472 --> 00:31:11.090
So take that conversation and
make it even better.

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.430
So I know all of that about you.

00:31:13.430 --> 00:31:17.660
So why can't I call you if you're
a traveling, road warrior person,

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:22.266
why can't I call you on a Thursday
evening when you're leaving on a Friday?

00:31:22.266 --> 00:31:24.560
And what are the stressors, right?

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:29.590
Traffic, parking, security,
upgrades, all of those things right?

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:31.730
Are you on time, all that sort of stuff.

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:36.503
Well, I have enough data to be able
to proactively call you on a Thursday

00:31:36.503 --> 00:31:38.733
afternoon and say hey, ma'am,

00:31:38.733 --> 00:31:44.084
I've seen that over the last five flights
we haven't been able to upgrade you.

00:31:44.084 --> 00:31:45.520
You're a valued customer.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:49.329
I want you to know that A,
you have your upgrade, B and

00:31:49.329 --> 00:31:51.540
I'm working with TSA on this.

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:53.644
I'm going to get you through
security in a different line.

00:31:53.644 --> 00:31:58.625
And by the way, because we're partners
with Uber and Lyft, here's a code for

00:31:58.625 --> 00:32:03.545
your Uber so that you have a car, you
have security and you have your upgrade.

00:32:03.545 --> 00:32:06.180
The night before you're going on a flight.

00:32:06.180 --> 00:32:08.130
How better do you sleep that night?

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:12.470
Now, again, for all of you that
travel a lot, you'll recognize that.

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:16.720
But that, to me, is relevant tech, and
that's how you put all this big data and

00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:19.060
management, thereof,
how you put it to use.

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:22.610
Not for this grandiose,
how do I extract more revenue from you?

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:25.730
Which is important and
we'll do that concurrently.

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:27.474
But more importantly, I want you to-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:29.982
&gt;&gt; It's a business school, right?

00:32:29.982 --> 00:32:33.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But again, it's a win-win, right?

00:32:33.730 --> 00:32:37.760
And when I say I want you to feel
better about flying with me,

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:39.660
that's something that
you'll feel better at.

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:42.520
[INAUDIBLE] Hey listen, I was kind of
pissed because I haven't been upgraded,

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:46.230
all of a sudden they called ahead of time,
knew what was going on, helped me out.

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:49.130
So that's a vision that
we're working towards.

00:32:49.130 --> 00:32:53.460
And so technology is a great
advantage in all those places.

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:55.010
It has to scale.

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:56.390
We are massive.

00:32:56.390 --> 00:32:58.410
Just so big, it's not even funny.

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:01.800
So we have to make sure we do,
we're able to work with some.

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:03.700
And the association is great.

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:05.780
We've gotta have Boeing,
Microsoft, and everyone else.

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:08.460
But we're always looking for
someone else, too.

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:12.720
And so it's exciting to see what
excitement's going on around here.

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:16.360
&gt;&gt; So you mentioned your platform, the
platform that you've been given as the CEO

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:21.380
of United, and I want to transition
to talking about some of the larger

00:33:21.380 --> 00:33:25.000
things out in the world that you have
played a role in having conversations

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:29.790
about including the fact that United is
a leader and its efforts to reduce carbon

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:33.870
emissions and recently committed
to having it's emissions by 2050.

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:38.376
But a recent UN climate report
suggests that we only have until 2030

00:33:38.376 --> 00:33:43.660
before we start to see the catastrophic
and irreversible effects to warming.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:49.350
So you are a leader but can and
should you do more to reduce an offset?

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:51.860
&gt;&gt; I'l start with a humorous
approach back to my SNL.

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:53.910
Michael Shay said this.

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:55.020
He goes have you heard?

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:58.975
The planet's dying in ten years and
then he says why don't I care?

00:33:58.975 --> 00:34:06.590
[LAUGH] We don't, and
he goes to say humorously.

00:34:06.590 --> 00:34:09.720
Because you can't say
everything's going to die

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:13.470
because no one cares about everything,
they care things that are all on me.

00:34:13.470 --> 00:34:15.860
You should watch it, it's pretty funny.

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:18.520
I'll tell you my evolution on this.

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:20.400
Personally and really as a company.

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:24.927
First and foremost,
we burn four billion, now-

00:34:24.927 --> 00:34:26.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:26.910 --> 00:34:29.400
&gt;&gt; Gallons of jet fuel, not regular fuel,

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:31.320
jet fuel a year.

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:37.290
So carbon footprint, dude, I have a
giant-size whatever issue in that regard.

00:34:37.290 --> 00:34:41.510
I also made a business, and
a lot of money for a lot of investors,

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:46.770
moving fossil fuels, coal, for
instance in the railroad space.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:51.070
And at that time it was sort
of my bias to disregard or

00:34:51.070 --> 00:34:54.959
disprove the concept of global
climate change and any risk thereof.

00:34:56.170 --> 00:35:00.620
I've evolved, I mean,
I still have a big carbon footprint.

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:05.490
I just see what we are doing as a planet.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:09.750
You just can't deny the fact that the
hardware, whether it's industrialization,

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:14.090
all the different things that we're
doing are creating a bigger risk.

00:35:14.090 --> 00:35:17.190
This planet will burst at some point
in time, I don't know when it is.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:20.680
So the questions Al Gore,
in my conversations with him, are simple.

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:23.770
He says do you believe in climate change?

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:24.990
Can you do something about it?

00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:26.930
And will you do something about it?

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:30.770
And so for me, and I think for
our company, do we believe it's real?

00:35:30.770 --> 00:35:32.030
Yeah, I think so.

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:37.150
Yeah I can argue the cyclical
nature of global warming, and

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:40.090
cooling over many centuries and
all of that.

00:35:40.090 --> 00:35:44.550
But the difference is man made emissions,
it all becoming more prevalent, and

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:46.050
more impactful.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.580
So yes, I believe in it,
we believe in it as a company.

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:50.690
Can you do something about it,
it's like I don't know,

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:53.830
we burn four billions of fuel
can we really do something?

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:55.600
It's like, yes you can.

00:35:55.600 --> 00:35:58.100
So bio-fuels are in their infancy but

00:35:58.100 --> 00:36:01.690
we're the leading folks to invest
in companies that manufacture it.

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:06.845
We just flew from here to Zurich
a couple of weeks ago on a fossil fuel.

00:36:06.845 --> 00:36:09.225
It isn't quite scale or economic enough.

00:36:09.225 --> 00:36:14.885
But our 2050 mission, to your question,
it's going to take a while.

00:36:14.885 --> 00:36:17.715
But will you do something about it?

00:36:17.715 --> 00:36:20.565
I, we are choosing that
that's going to be done.

00:36:20.565 --> 00:36:26.225
And what's cool about it, because
California anointed surfing as the state

00:36:26.225 --> 00:36:30.090
sport which I did a lot of that so
I was very happy.

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:33.040
So our president Jana Lampkins
here had a great idea,

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:34.880
why don't we fly surfboards free?

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:39.480
And as a complete different gesture,
guess who picks up on that?

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:40.890
There you go, somebody.

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:44.350
And now for the skiers,
we'll have to work on that.

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:50.430
But, it's a long way away, but
you gotta start somewhere.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:56.010
And by me doing it in this industry,
hopefully it a lot of

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:59.310
heavy carbon footprint companies
to begin to ask the question,

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:02.460
do you believe, can you do something,
and will you do something about it.

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:03.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.640
Immigration has also been a hot button
issue, and you have been vocal.

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:08.650
You have come out in support of DACA.

00:37:08.650 --> 00:37:12.770
You also refuse to let the US government
fly immigrant children who had

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:16.710
been separated from their families
at the border on any United flight.

00:37:16.710 --> 00:37:20.800
What responsibilities to executives of
public companies have when it comes to

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:22.940
taking a stand on these social and
political issues?

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:26.940
&gt;&gt; I mention the profitable but
principled earlier.

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:33.930
My opinion and I do it on the basis of
the strength of what we've been doing

00:37:33.930 --> 00:37:38.630
in the business, and creating shareholder
value, creating customer value.

00:37:38.630 --> 00:37:40.910
And on that foundation of accomplishment,
and

00:37:40.910 --> 00:37:43.520
success, in the terms that some
people want to look at it.

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:50.510
I see a responsibility, we see a
responsibility, my employees see a duty by

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:57.030
the nature of shared purpose of connecting
people and uniting the world, and

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:03.940
I have every freedom from our corporation
to do what we think is the right thing.

00:38:03.940 --> 00:38:09.550
And what's hard about it is that
it is not without its issues.

00:38:09.550 --> 00:38:12.690
You take a position on anything,
now a days,

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:16.960
and the social media world
lights up with negative.

00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:19.520
Haters are going to hate,
and boy, do they.

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:25.070
And so immigration and
things like that, because of my

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:29.240
heritage become identity politics,
right, so you quickly discontinued.

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:32.690
Things are taken out of
context all the time.

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:38.030
But you know what, if we're not building,
and showing our next generation

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:42.840
of leaders, that you are in this room,
that you should be certainly profitable,

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:46.450
but principled, that we shouldn't
think about those social causes?

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:49.780
Regardless of what they are, whatever
extreme that you can't voice it and

00:38:49.780 --> 00:38:54.990
I'm always very careful to not make
pronouncements that are my bias.

00:38:54.990 --> 00:38:58.610
If it's my opinion you will
know that it's my opinion.

00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:03.060
But there's just it's we teach a lot
of things ,ethics, morals and values.

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:06.830
I'm telling you the intersection that you
come to a fork in the road where you have

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:10.170
to make a decision on
something is a difficult one.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:12.020
And it's situational.

00:39:12.020 --> 00:39:17.600
I don't have a problem with guns or
anything, just whatever your choice is.

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:22.870
But when there's a shooting, and
one of your pilot's family member loses

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:28.930
a daughter who's 14 years old,
I can't stand by and say nothing.

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.340
I won't stand by and say nothing.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:35.270
And despite one person came to our
annual meeting and just berated me.

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:36.500
Warren Buffett said this.

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:39.200
How dare you as a CEO
take a personal approach.

00:39:39.200 --> 00:39:40.210
And you said it was.

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:42.540
I said,
it had nothing to do with politics.

00:39:42.540 --> 00:39:44.250
It had nothing to do with guns.

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:46.160
That was purely personal.

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:47.580
Somebody lost a loved one.

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:48.970
I can't stand by.

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:51.370
I won't stand by, and the team,

00:39:51.370 --> 00:39:54.550
the family that I belong to
won't let that happen either.

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:56.890
And so, I think we need more of that.

00:39:56.890 --> 00:40:01.010
Thoughtful, not so much identity product,
not so much But you know what,

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:05.200
on the right things we have a big
crisis coming with this caravan coming.

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:08.770
I mean the social and
economic instability around us is awful.

00:40:08.770 --> 00:40:10.420
This world is interconnected.

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:12.580
You know that because you're
growing up in an environment.

00:40:12.580 --> 00:40:15.940
It's so interconnected that something
happens, it's going to affect the US and

00:40:15.940 --> 00:40:17.730
there's going to be all hell to pay.

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:21.050
So we will continue to work with all
the right folks to see anything that we

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:22.080
can add to that.

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:24.500
The political issues,
immigration policies.

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:26.450
We'll let politicians manage with that.

00:40:26.450 --> 00:40:28.720
I'm just there to pick up
whatever pieces I can.

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:31.820
And I have the full support of
my united family and our board.

00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:33.590
And I think that's important to have.

00:40:33.590 --> 00:40:36.650
But, again, importantly,
you build that level of credibility.

00:40:36.650 --> 00:40:39.770
You build that goodwill by doing
the things that you're all learning to do

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:40.660
here, right?

00:40:40.660 --> 00:40:43.660
Strategy, involvement, leadership,
all those sorts of things.

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:46.260
But there's such a big human
component to all of this.

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:51.150
And don't ever forget that, know who you
are, put yourself in the right situations,

00:40:51.150 --> 00:40:54.800
because you will face decisions
in your life like I said before,

00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:56.440
where you have to choose.

00:40:56.440 --> 00:40:59.960
And how you choose, and when you choose,
there will be detractors,

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:01.070
there's no question.

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:04.410
But so damn the torpedos,
let's just do it.

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:04.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:41:06.080 --> 00:41:09.792
&gt;&gt; I mean I am fortunate to be in that
position that what are you going to do?

00:41:09.792 --> 00:41:13.258
[LAUGH] I mean, it's like you send me home
to my family that I don't see very often?

00:41:13.258 --> 00:41:14.489
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Gosh.

00:41:14.489 --> 00:41:18.015
[LAUGH] Well, my wife probably has
something to say about that, but.

00:41:18.015 --> 00:41:18.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So

00:41:18.660 --> 00:41:22.120
I want to sneak in one more quick question
before we turn it over to the audience.

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:23.690
&gt;&gt; I hear we have a class
coming in here soon.

00:41:23.690 --> 00:41:24.550
&gt;&gt; So, yeah.

00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:29.340
One of the questions we often
reflect on here at the GSP and

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:32.730
that I think a lot about is this question
of why should people follow you?

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:35.690
&gt;&gt; How do you respond to that question?

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:39.560
&gt;&gt; Why should people follow you?

00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:44.700
I think,
I have all these little silly sayings.

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:46.210
I always say proof, not promise.

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:48.188
It's like listen,

00:41:48.188 --> 00:41:50.412
I'll stand in front of you, I'm going to
tell you what we're going to do.

00:41:50.412 --> 00:41:54.421
And then you don't have to do a darn thing
Because all you're going to see is proof

00:41:54.421 --> 00:41:56.070
of what I just told you.

00:41:56.070 --> 00:41:58.320
And for the time I've been here,

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:02.770
I can honestly stand in front of my entire
United family and our share owners.

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:07.710
Increasingly in front of our customers
as we do more in that space.

00:42:07.710 --> 00:42:12.630
I had said is there anything ever I have
told you that we haven't delivered on?

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:16.480
It's hard to say that, but

00:42:16.480 --> 00:42:19.080
I don't say things publically that
we're not going to deliver on.

00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:22.470
We're very careful and
review everything we're going to say.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:23.900
It's important.

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:27.160
That's why people follow you
because they talk to each other.

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:29.600
You telling someone,
you need to follow me because I'm the man.

00:42:29.600 --> 00:42:30.440
I'm the boss.

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:31.240
Or I'm the smarter.

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:33.090
I did all these things
to this other company.

00:42:33.090 --> 00:42:34.840
All that's part of the equation.

00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:40.150
But every human needs a personal
connection, a personal desire.

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:44.600
A heart and a mind connection that
drives someone to what I generally call

00:42:45.680 --> 00:42:47.000
discretionary effort.

00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:50.700
This is a business where, I have,
they're spread all over the world.

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:55.580
A gate agent back in the back
corner of some airport in Turkey,

00:42:55.580 --> 00:42:59.940
I have no control over her and
what she's doing.

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:04.230
All I have is the communications that
we've done, the feeling of family,

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:06.990
the shared purpose and mission,
all of those superlatives and

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:09.230
all those things that keep
people doing business.

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:11.990
Because then, that person,
just like when raising children.

00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:15.807
All your parents have let you go, mostly.

00:43:15.807 --> 00:43:19.930
[LAUGH] And
you're left to make your own decisions.

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:21.290
Are you making the right decisions?

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:22.500
Some yes, some not.

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:25.420
And so, the why you should follow
me is not a question I ask.

00:43:25.420 --> 00:43:31.190
It's like, let me show you, let me prove
to you, and then people follow in droves.

00:43:31.190 --> 00:43:32.190
I mean they really do,

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:35.680
it's a really powerful thing when you
capture the hearts and minds of folks.

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:37.058
How you do it versus how I do it,

00:43:37.058 --> 00:43:40.344
that's what you have to figure out because
there is no one way of doing it and you

00:43:40.344 --> 00:43:44.129
got a lot of talented folks, faculty that
will tell you all those different things.

00:43:44.129 --> 00:43:48.129
But my view is you gotta know
who you are and for God's sake,

00:43:48.129 --> 00:43:50.460
another thing I say is swing easy.

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:54.450
We try too hard sometimes.

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:59.720
I mean guys in particular get a deep
voice when you ask a question,

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:01.849
well let me tell you sir, relax.

00:44:01.849 --> 00:44:05.869
And I say swing easy because if you
play golf, in particular tennis,

00:44:05.869 --> 00:44:10.270
if you see the professionals,
they make that effort's effortless.

00:44:10.270 --> 00:44:12.230
Right, their swing is effortless.

00:44:12.230 --> 00:44:15.920
We in our career sometimes try a little
too hard, especially early on.

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:19.270
Be the person that someone pulls aside and
says hey,

00:44:19.270 --> 00:44:23.830
Oscar, you might want to
try this versus that.

00:44:23.830 --> 00:44:25.800
You got to be that person
that people are willing to.

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:30.821
If I have a guiding principle or fact of
something that's been done that I've been

00:44:30.821 --> 00:44:36.570
so fortunate to have is that across my
career, someone's always pulled you aside.

00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:39.530
Especially when you thought
you were all that, right?

00:44:39.530 --> 00:44:42.960
I'd been, I was the youngest of this,
and the youngest of that, and all that.

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:48.830
But then somebody, key point if I could,
one boss, it's a pointed story.

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:51.455
I'm sitting at a review,
I just, I'm a 26-year-old kid.

00:44:51.455 --> 00:44:56.395
I went from this level at one company,
to this level at Coca-Cola.

00:44:56.395 --> 00:44:58.815
And I'm just feeling everything.

00:44:58.815 --> 00:45:01.042
And I think everyone around
me is kind of stupid,

00:45:01.042 --> 00:45:03.000
because I'm the smartest
thing in the world.

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:07.153
Six months in, my boss pulls me in, we
have this performance review and he says,

00:45:07.153 --> 00:45:10.084
hey, you exceed our expectations,
I took a risk on you,

00:45:10.084 --> 00:45:12.007
you've been killing it really.

00:45:12.007 --> 00:45:14.222
You're crushing it,
before that word was important.

00:45:14.222 --> 00:45:16.347
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Then he says,

00:45:16.347 --> 00:45:18.527
and he closes kind of the book,
and he pulls aside,

00:45:18.527 --> 00:45:22.340
he goes but if I could tell you
something on a more personal basis.

00:45:22.340 --> 00:45:25.088
And I'm like, he wants me to date
his daughter or something, probably.

00:45:25.088 --> 00:45:28.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, he said, and this is critical.

00:45:28.660 --> 00:45:29.600
He said you know what?

00:45:29.600 --> 00:45:36.659
You're really good, but you're not yet
as good as you think you are.

00:45:36.659 --> 00:45:38.910
And I was like, excuse me?

00:45:38.910 --> 00:45:42.102
That sounded kind of negative.

00:45:42.102 --> 00:45:46.996
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You're not yet

00:45:46.996 --> 00:45:49.320
as good as you think you are.

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:52.370
And I walked out of there, went through
all those seven stages of anger,

00:45:52.370 --> 00:45:54.560
all those little things that people do.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:56.330
But it was critical.

00:45:56.330 --> 00:46:00.410
Along life, you've gotta find
the right person that has that desire.

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:01.990
Remember, sharing is caring.

00:46:01.990 --> 00:46:03.270
And that made a huge difference,

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:06.690
because I'd walked into an environment
where I thought I was all that.

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:10.856
I was the youngest, and well I recognized
that people around me were incredibly,

00:46:10.856 --> 00:46:14.380
deeply, knowledgeable and experienced.

00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:16.850
Where my energy could be better,

00:46:16.850 --> 00:46:21.159
in fact, it's guided many things
like that, so always make sure,

00:46:21.159 --> 00:46:25.020
and don't ask people, like, Hey,
tell me something bad about myself, right?

00:46:25.020 --> 00:46:26.130
You just gotta act that way.

00:46:27.270 --> 00:46:29.523
And find people, love people around you.

00:46:29.523 --> 00:46:33.790
Give them the liberty, give them
the liberty to be honest with you.

00:46:33.790 --> 00:46:37.930
You will find more often than not, when
somebody tells you something negative.

00:46:37.930 --> 00:46:41.880
And you go, hey honey,
this jerk at work said this about me and

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:43.872
your spouse will go well, honey.

00:46:43.872 --> 00:46:45.470
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:46:45.470 --> 00:46:47.258
&gt;&gt; It’s an important thing, I'm sorry.

00:46:47.258 --> 00:46:49.705
&gt;&gt; No, we’d like to take a few questions
from the audience, if that’s okay.

00:46:49.705 --> 00:46:51.184
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:46:57.331 --> 00:46:58.900
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Edward Silva.

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:02.560
Thank you, Oscar,
I’m an MBA student here at GSB.

00:47:02.560 --> 00:47:07.440
And my question is, where with
the United do the new, creative,

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:11.620
even crazy ideas come from, and what
